the eu's approach to fighting terrorism  took center stage at the first general  affairs council of the year following  january's events in paris  ministers underlined the importance of  seeking agreement on travel surveillance  in order to monitor movements of terror  suspects  latvian foreign minister ed garinkovics  told reporters after the meeting  we had a very broad discussion during  today's general affairs council on  terrorism and  all the necessary measures the european  union  must take and i have to say that there  have been a very broad agreement  in the general affairs council during  our discussion that we have to move  forward with passenger names register we  also had a very broad agreement  concerning  um let's say the need to address  terrorism in the  comprehensive  way  the council also approved an agreement  on new rules to prevent money laundering  and terrorist financing  ministers also discussed a revision of  the multi-annual financial framework  the aim is to allow the eu to transfer  21.1 billion euros of unused funds from  2014 for use over the next years  as chairman of the meeting rinkovics  expressed optimism that agreement  allowing member states to use the  investment funds as agreed in the mff  regulation could soon be reached  ministers cast an eye over march's eu  leaders meeting agenda which will focus  on energy union relations with ukraine  and russia and economic issues
 

